From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:06:03 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: dwc: gs201-cp: ask for the target link speed after the
 first L0

dw_pcie_setup_rc() ends by setting PORT_LOGIC_SPEED_CHANGE, without
which a DesignWare port trains to L0 at 2.5 GT/s and stays there
whatever the target in Link Control 2. Google's establish_link runs
dw_pcie_setup_rc(), so stock's modem boot loader link comes up at
Gen3; the root port setup copied from it here stopped one write short.

Since then the boot loader link to the modem has trained at Gen1 x2
on every boot ("link at Gen1, below Gen3: retrying" twice, then kept on
the last try), each retry with a PERST# cycle under the modem's boot
loader. The modem has booted at that speed but then never serviced its
IPC buffers (MODEM-PLAN M4.1-M4.1c), while stock and another Linux port
on the same phone, both at Gen3 x2, carry IPC within seconds of ONLINE.

Set the bit with the rest of the root port setup. For the ROM link the
target is Gen1, so nothing changes there.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
index e157878d3..737ee525c 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-gs201-cp.c
@@ -370,6 +370,14 @@ static void cp_setup_root_port(struct gs201_pcie_cp *cp)
 	 * was never sized or assigned.
 	 */
 	dw_pcie_writew_dbi(pci, PCI_CLASS_DEVICE, PCI_CLASS_BRIDGE_PCI);
+
+	/*
+	 * Training always reaches L0 at 2.5 GT/s first; the port moves to
+	 * the target speed only when told to.
+	 */
+	val = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, PCIE_LINK_WIDTH_SPEED_CONTROL);
+	val |= PORT_LOGIC_SPEED_CHANGE;
+	dw_pcie_writel_dbi(pci, PCIE_LINK_WIDTH_SPEED_CONTROL, val);
 }
 
 /* exynos_pcie_setup_rc() for EP_SAMSUNG_MODEM, plus Google's EQ-off write */
